WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.690
- [Narrator] Think of ballet:
Nutcracker maybe comes to mind

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:04.523
or Swan Lake

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.449
but, probably, not this.

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:10.866
(manic orchestral music)

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.470
In the 1920s in Germany,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.853
the Bauhaus design revolution took off,

00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:24.150
buildings like this were out,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.050
Minimalism was in.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.518
But beyond architecture,
many spheres of art

00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:32.023
were touched by the Bauhaus
school, including ballet.

00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:37.750
- Triadic ballet has been
created by Oskar Schlemmer,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.760
Oskar Schlemmer wanted to find a form

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.360
for new way of dancing.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.230
Bauhaus went down to the functionality,

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.980
that's why he also created costumes,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.240
which would throw away useless decoration:

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.255
the tiaras, the tutus,
the golden costumes.

00:00:57.255 --> 00:01:00.624
- [Narrator] The Avant-Garde
Ballet debuted in 1922,

00:01:00.624 --> 00:01:03.240
and it was made into a film adaptation

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.023
by the Bavaria Atelier in 1970;

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.100
then, in 1977, it was recreated,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.780
giving birth to the version,
which Ivan now directs.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.863
- [Ivan] So the costumes
relate to geometric forms;

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.203
that's why it's called
Triadic, three forms:

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.782
it's a square; it's a
circle; it's a triangle.

00:01:24.782 --> 00:01:26.980
There are three parts in the ballet:

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.310
it's the yellow part,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.133
pink and black.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.520
Yes, that's it.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.520
The costumes, Schlemmer wanted
them to restrict the dancer

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.810
not to have the complete freedom.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.960
Oskar Schlemmer spent lots of his money

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.680
investing into the costumes,

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.980
went so far that his
family, when needed money,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.497
he said, "Sorry guys, I have
to work on the costumes,

00:01:57.497 --> 00:02:00.157
"you'll have to eat a
little bit more modest."

00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:03.330
- [Narrator] And it seems like

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.870
Schlemmer's labor of love paid off.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.790
Even today the geometric costumes

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.930
combined with the robotic dance movements

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.631
gets the audience's attention.

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:15.100
- You could hear people
giggling in the darkness

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.933
because the movements,

00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:19.450
(audience laughing)

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.700
it's very successful, because
our audience can't believe

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.390
that this is 100 years old,

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.960
because it looks like
it has been done today.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.960
There you see the visionary
power of Oskar Schlemmer,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.150
and the ideas of Bauhaus,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.939
and that's what this ballet gives you.

00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:42.189
(audience applauding)

